          "distinct": true,
          "id": "166039dc359bb3d9062c419e9ae5460ee8856f86",
          "message": "fix: keep the Join Scan's ORDER BY when LIMIT is a parameter (#6555)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5574\n\n## What\n\n`VisibilityFilterExec` now declares that it keeps its input's row order,\nso DataFusion no longer removes a `Sort` below it.\n\n## Why\n\nOn a generic plan with a parameterized `LIMIT`, the Join Scan advertises\nits `ORDER BY` as pathkeys, so Postgres adds no Sort above it, and the\n`LIMIT` is only injected into the DataFusion plan at execution time. At\nplanning time the plan therefore has a plain `Sort` with no fetch, and\n`VisibilityFilterOptimizerRule` does not treat a plain sort as a\nbarrier, so it places the visibility check for `p` above it.\n\n`VisibilityFilterExec` copies its input's equivalence properties, so it\nclaims the input's ordering, but it did not override\n`maintains_input_order`, which defaults to `false`. `EnforceSorting`\ngoes by the latter: a `SortExec` whose parent does not keep its order\ncannot affect the output, so it is removed as unnecessary. The runtime\n`Limit` then takes the first rows in hash-join probe order.\n\nWith a literal `LIMIT` the plan has `SortExec: TopK(fetch=5)`, which the\nvisibility rule treats as a barrier and `EnforceSorting` cannot drop, so\nthat case was already correct.\n\n## How\n\n`maintains_input_order` returns `true`. The node filters each batch with\n`filter_record_batch` and the surviving rows keep their order, which is\nwhat its equivalence properties already state.\n\nPutting the runtime fetch on the existing `Sort` instead would not be\ncorrect: the visibility check for `p` sits above that sort, so invisible\nrows could take slots in the Top K.\n\n`TantivyDecodeExec` and `TantivyFetchExec` carry their input's ordering\nthe same way (`preserved_ordering`) and do not override\n`maintains_input_order` either. I could not build a Join Scan plan that\nputs one of them above a plain `Sort`, so I left them alone; happy to\nadd the same override there if you prefer.\n\n## Tests\n\n`issue_5574.sql` runs the reproduction from the issue on a generic plan,\nplus a parameterized `OFFSET`, and compares both with the\nliteral-`LIMIT` query.\n\nOn `main` at `b764474`, PostgreSQL 18 via pgrx:\n\n| | `main` | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| DataFusion plan | no `SortExec` | `SortExec: expr=[id@1 ASC NULLS\nLAST]` above the `HashJoinExec` |\n| `EXECUTE issue_5574_page(5)` | `1, 2, 4, 5, 7` | `1, 1, 2, 2, 4`, same\nas `LIMIT 5` |\n\nThe new test fails on `main` and passes with this change, on two\nconsecutive runs. Full `pg_regress` with the fix: 388 passed, 1 failed\n(`issue_3678`, which fails the same way on a clean `main` here). `cargo\nfmt --check` and `cargo clippy -p pg_search --all-targets -- -D\nwarnings` are clean.",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-29T17:36:45-07:00",
